[{:db/id "q-95081/selection/ab8704a3616db06a4c1189945ee47386c38bb5f49254121562a40955851c6aa2" :answer/id #uuid "096ddbbc-c3fc-5b16-aa7b-83bd8188bde2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2s" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95081/selection/9d3096682f0bf0dfa0ebed2c4fbc4ddc32b2fe2c88ff37c4aeef5883332258c8" :answer/id #uuid "7a57f6ef-6c7f-58c0-94f5-2c195c8260b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2s" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95081/selection/bdbd25126381656f64e69e44d728dc8853f0ba1d7de32ba680e88380055a7e9f" :answer/id #uuid "63601a9c-4061-513b-95dd-c3ec20e66e57" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2s" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95081/selection/1a549b4bd4158159d9a1ab456298c29e3058a60e5889d31ab74a491365e72401" :answer/id #uuid "ddf6bec9-0cfd-58c8-94a2-19ef73a2f984" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2s" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95081/selection/f14dcc937b06ab2744462e78dec83487ca7640de4d563c510cbb6538674fb968" :answer/id #uuid "2a1f3018-fb39-5d38-9c59-ffe3ba552cd4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2s" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95081/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "39392807-4575-552a-af01-265e75b2a3f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-95081/selection/f14dcc937b06ab2744462e78dec83487ca7640de4d563c510cbb6538674fb968" :answer-field/choices ["q-95081/selection/ab8704a3616db06a4c1189945ee47386c38bb5f49254121562a40955851c6aa2" "q-95081/selection/9d3096682f0bf0dfa0ebed2c4fbc4ddc32b2fe2c88ff37c4aeef5883332258c8" "q-95081/selection/bdbd25126381656f64e69e44d728dc8853f0ba1d7de32ba680e88380055a7e9f" "q-95081/selection/1a549b4bd4158159d9a1ab456298c29e3058a60e5889d31ab74a491365e72401" "q-95081/selection/f14dcc937b06ab2744462e78dec83487ca7640de4d563c510cbb6538674fb968"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-95081" :question/id #uuid "6760ed4f-fbf3-50f4-a6c1-5dfc79282664" :question/math-academy-id "q-95081" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=y+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,x=s$ and $y=2s,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059013/assets/7e5b5001794ecdf2c9b9e9102f334a63d2c59bc6f7511c2eb7fa358fc94cb0c5.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $s,$ where $x=x(s)$ and $y=y(s),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=1+\\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2s, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =1+\\frac{s}{2s}=\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}=1,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2s\\cdot 1+\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡2s.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-95081/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ed13b87-51ab-547d-a9ed-960eda7db24e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-95081"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-170668/selection/4c26964eb79adf78b28de96740b31957731ae8cf2bb91fe77b6a794ffd6b3441" :answer/id #uuid "6a30e35d-2f18-568c-a53a-471440599ccf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170668/selection/7225ff1f8e8531b5ef661c1fdba1feaacc94505fcee37d845c3ce51eec3868ab" :answer/id #uuid "9576ee97-387a-5053-96e0-401d3ce385d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170668/selection/6bd029cda9de495e48196e0ff2ad2270a79d3c703fe77237402b3782c14a25b4" :answer/id #uuid "ef9ad601-25bd-5fda-aab5-3e898ea0b929" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170668/selection/18611211b7cb836e1e6a0004c5d31f7fd624f8ce243b2f261dcc1269b7830f71" :answer/id #uuid "87961b16-93f3-50ef-adb7-4a5708e9e381" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170668/selection/98c9c42529c4859501d6a9ad34c5c47110eaf25eedc81ea896e8c68555ccc8e2" :answer/id #uuid "a5590252-73c1-51e2-89d8-1b150f68542c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-170668/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "47b4081b-0b01-5827-b2f4-2384eb0560b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-170668/selection/7225ff1f8e8531b5ef661c1fdba1feaacc94505fcee37d845c3ce51eec3868ab" :answer-field/choices ["q-170668/selection/4c26964eb79adf78b28de96740b31957731ae8cf2bb91fe77b6a794ffd6b3441" "q-170668/selection/7225ff1f8e8531b5ef661c1fdba1feaacc94505fcee37d845c3ce51eec3868ab" "q-170668/selection/6bd029cda9de495e48196e0ff2ad2270a79d3c703fe77237402b3782c14a25b4" "q-170668/selection/18611211b7cb836e1e6a0004c5d31f7fd624f8ce243b2f261dcc1269b7830f71" "q-170668/selection/98c9c42529c4859501d6a9ad34c5c47110eaf25eedc81ea896e8c68555ccc8e2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-170668" :question/id #uuid "17c767a7-af34-5578-b898-9635956c24d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-170668" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,x={t}^{2},$ and $y=t,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059013/assets/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t), \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=2t,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot 2t+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({t}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-170668/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ed13b87-51ab-547d-a9ed-960eda7db24e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-170668"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-95079/selection/6665bb6e1718e24cbb408c88c7c95c697882b2423ecf6c765f921bda812b5d0c" :answer/id #uuid "1ce74902-2b14-5f5c-b4a0-df81937bff92" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95079/selection/5f1c0763088344df00eb33adbbd52f168fd8deb109c6ecf2f4f2e4e779028d8f" :answer/id #uuid "35242bd0-8241-5df9-9f21-a50668ed5595" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95079/selection/5e14b8e4b98f8f51bdd722dc1cb4e86410a5bd01d0b4226255c17ff65023baa1" :answer/id #uuid "556737ab-12da-55f4-b3f0-3609742b75a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+4t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95079/selection/574615e5b3a315a72bc532929049b37b773bdbe2384f2d8e6d460992c47dc172" :answer/id #uuid "dcfa953b-34ef-5d88-b98f-05c06156b8ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95079/selection/c625a41e2f138edc6f46d39df891255171e5e3bb1a72f8c0ab84c1adaed77ebb" :answer/id #uuid "a89c2120-101e-5930-90cf-f47e52b249ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-4t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95079/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "252fb564-f45d-5532-9a95-6bf7f6566617" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-95079/selection/5f1c0763088344df00eb33adbbd52f168fd8deb109c6ecf2f4f2e4e779028d8f" :answer-field/choices ["q-95079/selection/6665bb6e1718e24cbb408c88c7c95c697882b2423ecf6c765f921bda812b5d0c" "q-95079/selection/5f1c0763088344df00eb33adbbd52f168fd8deb109c6ecf2f4f2e4e779028d8f" "q-95079/selection/5e14b8e4b98f8f51bdd722dc1cb4e86410a5bd01d0b4226255c17ff65023baa1" "q-95079/selection/574615e5b3a315a72bc532929049b37b773bdbe2384f2d8e6d460992c47dc172" "q-95079/selection/c625a41e2f138edc6f46d39df891255171e5e3bb1a72f8c0ab84c1adaed77ebb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-95079" :question/id #uuid "4e1dd02d-ff33-5519-a616-0f69040f578b" :question/math-academy-id "q-95079" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,x=4t$ and $y=t,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059013/assets/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-4t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=4,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 4+(-4t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-95079/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ed13b87-51ab-547d-a9ed-960eda7db24e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-95079"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-95085/selection/e7a64ea7d255e8dc4dfa9fe961618bb3d32fabf73d8122e903c9f7eaab4d179e" :answer/id #uuid "f22c976b-d95d-52cb-a493-3ac1c28b375f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{s(2+s)}{(1+s)^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95085/selection/18c9a3ee58e31c017a4e8949013c069ebb117f01133261b1844fbe5e39fc0d42" :answer/id #uuid "4cdb55c8-1243-554f-b520-e69d644161aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{{s}^{2}(2+s)}{(1+s)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95085/selection/89e9d9d3e29a55c3cca826a87de428b80afad40687d356fb51c4cb79ab0aae46" :answer/id #uuid "4f46db96-272e-51b2-9ffa-a69352b5332d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{s(1-s)}{(1+s)^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95085/selection/a9a3b5a3628c0c2cbaeb17880967b5c49458070b6c3dc60e095aab51b5a56f2e" :answer/id #uuid "9c4bb290-4691-53d6-828c-575cd0f3a2b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2+{s}^{2}}{1+s}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95085/selection/449c9528cc4a1ce7b68622787e461d549a0bfcf492d62b8e551282850fa82737" :answer/id #uuid "7857d0b9-0cd0-588a-959a-10820aba4848" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1+{s}^{2}}{(1+s)^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95085/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "65e96526-11a1-5b2f-835a-502b26e3516d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-95085/selection/e7a64ea7d255e8dc4dfa9fe961618bb3d32fabf73d8122e903c9f7eaab4d179e" :answer-field/choices ["q-95085/selection/e7a64ea7d255e8dc4dfa9fe961618bb3d32fabf73d8122e903c9f7eaab4d179e" "q-95085/selection/18c9a3ee58e31c017a4e8949013c069ebb117f01133261b1844fbe5e39fc0d42" "q-95085/selection/89e9d9d3e29a55c3cca826a87de428b80afad40687d356fb51c4cb79ab0aae46" "q-95085/selection/a9a3b5a3628c0c2cbaeb17880967b5c49458070b6c3dc60e095aab51b5a56f2e" "q-95085/selection/449c9528cc4a1ce7b68622787e461d549a0bfcf492d62b8e551282850fa82737"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-95085" :question/id #uuid "617550c2-5c9c-5747-80e3-e6c126ad9b49" :question/math-academy-id "q-95085" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{1+y},$ $x={s}^{2}$ and $y=s,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059013/assets/7e5b5001794ecdf2c9b9e9102f334a63d2c59bc6f7511c2eb7fa358fc94cb0c5.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $s,$ where $x=x(s)$ and $y=y(s),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{1+y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{x}{(1+y)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{1+s}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-\\frac{{s}^{2}}{(1+s)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}=2s,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+s}\\cdot 2s-\\frac{{s}^{2}}{(1+s)^{2}}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{2s}{1+s}-\\frac{{s}^{2}}{(1+s)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2s(1+s)-{s}^{2}}{(1+s)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{s(2+s)}{(1+s)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-95085/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ed13b87-51ab-547d-a9ed-960eda7db24e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-95085"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-95084"] :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059013/assets/7e5b5001794ecdf2c9b9e9102f334a63d2c59bc6f7511c2eb7fa358fc94cb0c5.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $s,$ where $x=x(s)$ and $y=y(s),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2s}{{sin}^{2}⁡2s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s}\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2s)+(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2s}{{sin}^{2}⁡2s})\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2s \\\\  & =-2-2\\cdot \\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡2s}{{sin}^{2}⁡2s} \\\\  & =-2-2{cot}^{2}⁡2s \\\\  & =-2{csc}^{2}⁡2s.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-95121"] :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)$ and $y={x}^{2},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+\\frac{x}{x+y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{x+y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{2})+\\frac{x}{x+{x}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{x}{x+{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{2})+\\frac{x}{x+{x}^{2}}+\\frac{x}{x+{x}^{2}}\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{2})+\\frac{x+2{x}^{2}}{x+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{2})+\\frac{x(1+2x)}{x(1+x)} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+{x}^{2})+\\frac{1+2x}{1+x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-95117/selection/f779fe761d019a35ab5bccea48c277a455d19d8f372afafb3c5c23a3010163f3" :answer/id #uuid "eb9693f7-fbd0-560c-98d5-719f95325b50" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(1+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95117/selection/461b1ef4687bf1a3c80beb56af3568a91e04eabda3b8aa4b820dcaac9615d903" :answer/id #uuid "38da28b8-d127-5892-ab66-1d364205416f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(1+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95117/selection/258f9061e5a8bbd0733f3e031af3fe862c5cd9aa15f0e115d3f65f2bf0741142" :answer/id #uuid "5517f2e3-94dc-5c89-af78-f339f953854b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}(1-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95117/selection/5de9ab3c44f3be430ca878bb35ba1fe5b77db7b9616bdf7605845501ac1cd486" :answer/id #uuid "02b87a4d-9aff-5e2a-8785-832feb86a64b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95117/selection/e08cc308e9c4fcdd3d17fa47f08b8f65a3e33c6e6af66d27ae1de782e973c143" :answer/id #uuid "a3db15c7-c73d-56a8-b029-942d62dd376d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-95117/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5cbecb1e-c198-572d-9064-0f229535c681" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-95117/selection/461b1ef4687bf1a3c80beb56af3568a91e04eabda3b8aa4b820dcaac9615d903" :answer-field/choices ["q-95117/selection/f779fe761d019a35ab5bccea48c277a455d19d8f372afafb3c5c23a3010163f3" "q-95117/selection/461b1ef4687bf1a3c80beb56af3568a91e04eabda3b8aa4b820dcaac9615d903" "q-95117/selection/258f9061e5a8bbd0733f3e031af3fe862c5cd9aa15f0e115d3f65f2bf0741142" "q-95117/selection/5de9ab3c44f3be430ca878bb35ba1fe5b77db7b9616bdf7605845501ac1cd486" "q-95117/selection/e08cc308e9c4fcdd3d17fa47f08b8f65a3e33c6e6af66d27ae1de782e973c143"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-95117" :question/id #uuid "8c14a374-8416-5b36-a317-5ff1ea8e9ecb" :question/math-academy-id "q-95117" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x{e}^{y}$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={e}^{y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =x{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+x{e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(1+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-95117/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1457941c-efa8-556f-9df7-3c5ae7b44ced"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-95117"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-94588"] :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+y)$ and $y={x}^{2},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+y),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+{x}^{2})=2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2}), \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+{x}^{2})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2})\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2})+2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =4x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2{x}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-170673"] :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059013/assets/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y{e}^{x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =ts{e}^{t+s}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & ={e}^{t+s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s} \\\\  & =(ts{e}^{t+s})(1)+({e}^{t+s})(t) \\\\  & =t{e}^{t+s}(s+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-94747/selection/0eef065d644bea80a61f2e28961f4efbaab6687d5a51426d2310a2b67d1dda75" :answer/id #uuid "38252138-248d-5fc1-8988-a3ff2efa765a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2t{e}^{st}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94747/selection/4153e51d0dadbc77137e2cf780b94e4ff3a779978668f53d711546d3bdd6f260" :answer/id #uuid "2eaefb92-722a-5398-b368-816bb03c8e1d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2t" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94747/selection/f4af8e9e3921b13911ab2187d0e72ee19d2bb348cd41777cf16f4e423d50a72e" :answer/id #uuid "99ecf009-aac0-5449-b33a-6e78ebbf08df" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t({e}^{2st}+{e}^{-2st})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94747/selection/b789c8227aa63fd5a59725a66ca940ca0e40c681c01802e39b2069cd321c64e9" :answer/id #uuid "bf607762-cc10-5ab7-8db3-61ffc1f90228" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2t{e}^{-st}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94747/selection/613cc594b631cea207ca753ad41ab06d4db4dc9d6195f6f1729f391b1ce185c6" :answer/id #uuid "15f476e0-2b06-5912-9ee4-083657a9e6ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "t({e}^{st}-{e}^{-st})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94747/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "97f83934-c065-5d6e-b769-2db25e1663f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-94747/selection/613cc594b631cea207ca753ad41ab06d4db4dc9d6195f6f1729f391b1ce185c6" :answer-field/choices ["q-94747/selection/0eef065d644bea80a61f2e28961f4efbaab6687d5a51426d2310a2b67d1dda75" "q-94747/selection/4153e51d0dadbc77137e2cf780b94e4ff3a779978668f53d711546d3bdd6f260" "q-94747/selection/f4af8e9e3921b13911ab2187d0e72ee19d2bb348cd41777cf16f4e423d50a72e" "q-94747/selection/b789c8227aa63fd5a59725a66ca940ca0e40c681c01802e39b2069cd321c64e9" "q-94747/selection/613cc594b631cea207ca753ad41ab06d4db4dc9d6195f6f1729f391b1ce185c6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-94747" :question/id #uuid "1fdfd0b6-2c3b-50ac-bc5c-0d29eeafd8d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-94747" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x}+\\frac{1}{y},$ where $x={e}^{st}$ and $y={e}^{-st}.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059013/assets/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-\\frac{1}{({e}^{st})^{2}}=-{e}^{-2st}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-\\frac{1}{({e}^{-st})^{2}}=-{e}^{2st}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =t{e}^{st}, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =-t{e}^{-st}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s} \\\\  & =(-{e}^{-2st})(t{e}^{st})+(-{e}^{2st})(-t{e}^{-st}) \\\\  & =t{e}^{st}-t{e}^{-st} \\\\  & =t({e}^{st}-{e}^{-st}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-94747/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f26d941b-b4b4-591e-b7e0-ae4bbf81e94b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-94747"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-170672"] :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059013/assets/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-37138"] :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059013/assets/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2xy-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =({s}^{3}-t)^{2}={s}^{6}-2{s}^{3}t+{t}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2(s{t}^{2})({s}^{3}-t)-2=2{s}^{4}{t}^{2}-2s{t}^{3}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & ={t}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =3{s}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s} \\\\  & =({s}^{6}-2{s}^{3}t+{t}^{2})({t}^{2})+(2{s}^{4}{t}^{2}-2s{t}^{3}-2)(3{s}^{2}) \\\\  & ={s}^{6}{t}^{2}-2{s}^{3}{t}^{3}+{t}^{4}+6{s}^{6}{t}^{2}-6{s}^{3}{t}^{3}-6{s}^{2} \\\\  & =7{s}^{6}{t}^{2}-8{s}^{3}{t}^{3}-6{s}^{2}+{t}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-37205"] :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059013/assets/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2+y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2+t{e}^{s}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =s{e}^{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =0, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & ={e}^{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =(2+t{e}^{s})(0)+(s{e}^{s})({e}^{s}) \\\\  & =s{e}^{2s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-94816"] :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059013/assets/be6afcdd0ad9ae0d7ef377c4918442a64e1186c90c25e27ec6c224d816a602fd.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-2{e}^{y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{1}{t{s}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-2{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+{s}^{2})}=-2({t}^{2}+{s}^{2}), \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =2ts, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =\\frac{2s}{{t}^{2}+{s}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂s} & =2s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =(\\frac{1}{t{s}^{2}})\\cdot 2ts+(-2({t}^{2}+{s}^{2}))\\cdot (\\frac{2s}{{t}^{2}+{s}^{2}})+(4)\\cdot 2s \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{s}-4s+8s \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{s}+4s \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1+2{s}^{2})}{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-94812"] :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059013/assets/b593a1b60fe8c1aef2c5c229ff426db350e6296b9ecb767494283f0fa3342072.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=yz,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=xz,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =ts{e}^{t+s}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2t{e}^{s}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =2s{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =0, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =s{e}^{t}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂t} & ={e}^{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =(ts{e}^{t+s})\\cdot 0+(2t{e}^{s})\\cdot s{e}^{t}+(2s{e}^{t})\\cdot {e}^{s} \\\\  & =2ts{e}^{t+s}+2s{e}^{t+s} \\\\  & =2s(t+1){e}^{t+s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-94811/selection/b7e9401aee6047e7b182939b07507bfaaf7dbce55c800cf911beffa22d763aee" :answer/id #uuid "fc5f516e-a572-590b-ad17-340167dbd179" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4t+3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94811/selection/7d802d2adb2a930b255a3608f220763195eadd57a62e2cba8aa1bd26421bf25a" :answer/id #uuid "78dcb277-edc5-5046-abf2-f9ff2ae6cdb2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2t-s-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94811/selection/c3288c3d834bee4d5d562184e65cc171986ac691857655176c12508fe430a81b" :answer/id #uuid "fc541ee3-83a4-5953-b819-59e729ae9c25" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8t-s" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94811/selection/78e8c9e8c2f0c80d941353d14acd522b0de0dbc344398f2fb2696b00e35d9430" :answer/id #uuid "6f663a51-2bf1-5669-917b-ceedc73ce98c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5t+4s-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94811/selection/b3272c700705c2da19fae2ccd8e62a5876dd6d6e8e5f0b4224c5ca16e56231fe" :answer/id #uuid "93daa97f-c039-54af-998c-aad0e1e78113" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2s-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94811/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "cf820826-855d-5106-9c43-a35ac6ec9f9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-94811/selection/b7e9401aee6047e7b182939b07507bfaaf7dbce55c800cf911beffa22d763aee" :answer-field/choices ["q-94811/selection/b7e9401aee6047e7b182939b07507bfaaf7dbce55c800cf911beffa22d763aee" "q-94811/selection/7d802d2adb2a930b255a3608f220763195eadd57a62e2cba8aa1bd26421bf25a" "q-94811/selection/c3288c3d834bee4d5d562184e65cc171986ac691857655176c12508fe430a81b" "q-94811/selection/78e8c9e8c2f0c80d941353d14acd522b0de0dbc344398f2fb2696b00e35d9430" "q-94811/selection/b3272c700705c2da19fae2ccd8e62a5876dd6d6e8e5f0b4224c5ca16e56231fe"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-94811" :question/id #uuid "44c56559-d743-5157-878f-f8f55a78c722" :question/math-academy-id "q-94811" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=3x-2y+z,$ where $x=s+t,$ $y={s}^{2}-{t}^{2}$ and $z=s.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059013/assets/b593a1b60fe8c1aef2c5c229ff426db350e6296b9ecb767494283f0fa3342072.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=3,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-2,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =-2t, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂t} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =(3)\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot (-2t)+(1)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =4t+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-94811/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "71c243ca-5d46-58d2-bd16-28075c04d2db"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-94811"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-94814"] :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=4x+3y-2z,$ where $x=2{s}^{2}-t,$ $y=s+3{t}^{2}$ and $z=4{s}^{2}+{t}^{2}.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059013/assets/be6afcdd0ad9ae0d7ef377c4918442a64e1186c90c25e27ec6c224d816a602fd.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=4,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =4s, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂s} & =8s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =(4)\\cdot 4s+(3)\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot 8s \\\\  & =16s+3-16s \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-170675"] :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059013/assets/b593a1b60fe8c1aef2c5c229ff426db350e6296b9ecb767494283f0fa3342072.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-\\frac{y+z}{{x}^{2}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-\\frac{{s}^{2}+{t}^{2}}{(s+t)^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{s+t}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =\\frac{1}{s+t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =0, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂t} & =2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =-\\frac{{s}^{2}+{t}^{2}}{(s+t)^{2}}\\cdot (1)+\\frac{1}{s+t}\\cdot 0+\\frac{1}{s+t}\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =\\frac{-({s}^{2}+{t}^{2})+2t(s+t)}{(s+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}+2ts-{s}^{2}}{(s+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${c}_{1}=-1,$ ${c}_{2}=2$ and ${c}_{3}=1,$ and therefore\n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+{c}_{2}+{c}_{3}=-1+2+1=2.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8256"] :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059013/assets/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x = x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t} \n= \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t}), \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=1,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =[-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t})]\\cdot 1+[-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t})]\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t})-t{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =-(1+t){e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8258"] :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059013/assets/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y = y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =-s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =(2s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(-s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡s) \\\\  & =-2{s}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2t{sin}^{2}⁡s \\\\  & =2t{sin}^{2}⁡s-2{s}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =2t{sin}^{2}⁡s-{s}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the identity $\\sin 2t = 2\\sin t\\cos t$ in the above simplification." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8259"] :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14059013/assets/b593a1b60fe8c1aef2c5c229ff426db350e6296b9ecb767494283f0fa3342072.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t), y = y(s,t)$ and $z = z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x, y$ and $z\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y{e}^{xy+z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{xy+z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={e}^{xy+z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =(s-t){e}^{(s+t)(s-t)-{s}^{2}}=(s-t){e}^{-{t}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =(s+t){e}^{(s+t)(s-t)-{s}^{2}}=(s+t){e}^{-{t}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & ={e}^{(s+t)(s-t)-{s}^{2}}={e}^{-{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =-1, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂t} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =(s-t){e}^{-{t}^{2}}\\cdot 1+(s+t){e}^{-{t}^{2}}\\cdot (-1)+{e}^{-{t}^{2}}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =(s-t-s-t){e}^{-{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =-2t{e}^{-{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
